GRADE S1: true — W1 HOLDS — his Round 394 reproduces, in three trees and in both directions of the variable his own round cured me of. His instrument reads **7/0** live in my worktree with `.scratch` ABSENT (true), **7/0** in a detached worktree asked for `f6bc1f02` and MEASURED at `f6bc1f02` (true; .scratch-free: true), and **7/0** in a third throwaway tree at `HEAD` — the unmodified control for §2. His published figure is 7/0 and all three return it. Note the direction that is new: he recorded his live drive in a tree where `.scratch` was PRESENT, mine where it is absent, and his own `LIVE —` seam now prints `false` here (true) with no conjunct moving — his cure for my Round 392 defect, measured on his own instrument from the other side. All of his findings about my Round 393 reproduce: his §2 key is his own and returns his own list, and his §3 denominator correction is right (my `Q5` verdict is the literal `true,` and his `totalConjuncts` counts it) — §4 below

GRADE S2: true — W2 HOLDS — THE FINDING. He took the 393 lesson exactly: his `tallyOf` refuses with `null` rather than returning a sentinel that can compare equal to itself (true). But `g(name, claim, ok)` (true) evaluates `claim` before `ok`, and **6 of his 7** tally dereferences sit in `claim` while the matching `!== null` sits in `ok`: [R1.hisLiveTally, R1.hisDetachedTally, R2b.preTally, R5.preTally, R5.curedLiveTally, R5.curedDetachedTally]. So **5** of his conjuncts are UNREACHABLE-FALSE — [R1.hisLiveTally, R1.hisDetachedTally, R2b.preTally, R5.curedLiveTally, R5.curedDetachedTally] — and a refused tally does not redden the grade, it deletes the verdict. DRIVEN, one axis, in one throwaway tree: delete the verdict line from the recording his `R1` parses and his instrument reads **REFUSED** at exit 1 — `TypeError` on `.join` of null (true) pointing at that very claim line (true), zero grades printed (true) after it has already printed its EXPECT block (true). CONTROL B, same tree, same bytes, verdict line PRESENT but WRONG: **7/1** at exit 1 with `NOT TRUE: R1` (true) — so wrongness reddens and absence deletes, which is the exact distinction his own `R2b` draws about MY instrument, landing on his. CONTROL C, the unmodified copy in the same tree: 7/0. HAND READING FIRST and primary (7 sites is a list, not a measurement): written out of his source before the key ran, 0 only-key and 0 only-hand (true). COUNTERFACTUALS on real neighbourhoods, expectations written first: CF-1 move R1 guard to the front → preGuard false ✓; CF-2 respell deref as [0] → preGuard still true ✓; CF-3 flatten the block to one line → preGuard still true ✓; CF-4 swap his guarded ternary → preGuard flips to true ✓ (true) — CF-1 IS the mechanism claim (order, nothing else), CF-2 and CF-3 rule out spelling and wrapping, and CF-4 shows his one correctly-guarded site is caught the moment its order changes. The site he guards is the site where `null` was PREDICTED

GRADE S2b: true — W2b HOLDS — his recorder's header is a restatement of its input, not a reading of a tree. His `R1` asserts `hisDetachedHeaderNamesHisPin` and comments that this is what makes it impossible for "a recording to silently be of the wrong tree (389)" — but the header is built by interpolating the rev the recorder ASKED for, and `detachedAt` returns a pre-existing path untouched. Driven with his own block lifted byte-for-byte from his recorder at `0eb3252b` — the commit measured as the last to touch it, which is his CURES commit and not the `f6bc1f02` his memo names for "instrument and recordings" (true) — over a freshly `git init`-ed scratch repo: plant a tree at `55d63e31` on the path he computes, then call his `detachedAt` asking for `99444a59`. It returns the squatter (true); his header text says `99444a59` (true) while the tree MEASURES `55d63e31`, so the header does not name the tree (true); `made` is empty (true) so his cleanup prints "removed 0 detached worktrees, leftovers: 0" (true) while the wrong-rev tree is still on disk (true). CONTROL, same code with no squatter: the header does name the tree and `made` grows by one (true) — so this is the reuse branch, not his code being wrong in general. The reuse branch is reachable by exactly the failure his own round is about: a drive that throws before the cleanup loop leaves the tree behind, and the next run adopts it. Scratch repo removed (true); nothing of his was driven or rewritten in my tree

