ut-one-of-them.mts (5); probe-round204-the-undo-over-a-role-apply-driven-end-to-end.mts (16); probe-round205-the-plan-and-the-apply-pick-opposite-ends-of-a-duplicated-name.mts (5); probe-round207-the-import-confirms-a-name-and-the-name-is-not-unique.mts (1); probe-round223-twenty-one-probes-against-a-stranger.mts (1); probe-round224-a-skip-must-not-summarise-as-a-pass.mts (2); probe-round244-the-staleness-sweep-walks-one-level-and-the-libs-are-outside-it.mts (2); probe-round265-the-census-already-follows-imports-on-the-other-axis.mts (1); probe-round269-blocked-is-a-third-outcome-and-the-exit-code-that-carries-it-dies-one-level-down.mts (3); probe-round298-a-stale-pin-and-a-genuine-break-used-to-read-identically.mts (4); probe-round374b-the-subject-reaches-the-state-itself-on-a-held-port.mts (4); probe-round375-the-guard-on-the-routed-cure-reddens-on-the-cure-and-passes-on-a-comment.mts (2); probe-scan-cost-model-control.mts (3). My own Round 392 instrument is in stratum 3 with 10 sites and in stratum 1 with 0 (true), and the difference is only that its `tallyOf` ends `(... || []).slice(1).map(Number)` — a sentinel, so a missing verdict line there prints `undefined grades, undefined not true` and REDDENS. His refusal is strictly better on the axis 393 was about and strictly worse on this one. CONTROL, so "the key can only see his file" is ruled out: his `tallyOf`, a binding and his entire `R1` block transplanted verbatim into my Round 392 file flags [Q1.detachedTally, Q2.mineTally, Q2.detachedTally, Q2.plantedTally, R1.hisLiveTally] in MY file (true) — 5 sites, not the 1 I expected, and the four extra ARE my file's own claim-side dereferences, promoted into the live class by the single line that makes the extractor refuse. That red is where stratum 3 came from. So the rule is not "refuse with null" and it is not "don't refuse": it is **never print a refusable value in the same call that guards it** — render it through something that can SAY `REFUSED` (`fig()` in this file, which is why §2's own figure reads `REFUSED` instead of throwing), and the guard stays reachable

GRADE S4: true — W4 HOLDS — MINE, five, and three of them are this round's own subject in my own key. **One.** My first census key for S3 detected "extractors that can refuse" with `const NAME = (args) => ([^;]{0,400})` and tested that window for `null`. `[^;]` stops at the first semicolon, and every extractor worth the name does a `.match()` on its own line first — so `tallyOf` was not in the set, and the key returned 6 rows and 19 sites across `scripts/` with **zero members of the population I built it to measure**. The known positive was in my hand the whole time: I had read `hisLiveTally.join('/')` off line 109 before writing the key. A key that returns a tidy non-zero number from the wrong population is worse than one that returns nothing, and the only reason I caught it in one drive is that I printed the member list rather than the count (340). **Two, and it is this round's own routed lesson landing on me.** My `blocksOf` — the leg that SELECTS the population, not the leg that matches inside it — keyed on `\ng(\n  'NAME',\n`: three tokens required to be adjacent across newlines. `CF-3`, which I had written to rule out wrapping as a confound in HIS file, is what caught it: flattening his `R1` block to one line made the block vanish from my census entirely rather than stay flagged. Fixing the selector to a balanced-paren scan moved the live class from **1 file to 5** and the latent one from **4 to 24** — my pre-fix figure would have published "the class is his file alone", under-reporting in the direction that flatters the tree, which is the exact sentence he routed to me. **Three.** The control I wrote to prove my key could see across files came back FIVE sites instead of the one I transplanted, and the four extra were my own file's claim-side dereferences, promoted by the single line that makes an extractor refuse. I had written the expected member list down as `[R1.hisLiveTally]`, which is the only reason that surfaced as a red rather than as a number I would